The Security Council,
Recalling all its previous resolutions, statements of its President and press statements on the situation in Mali, in particular resolutions 2374 (2017), 2480 (2019), 2584 (2021), 2649 (2022) and 2690 (2023),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity and independence of Mali, and to the principles of non-interference and good-neighbourliness,
Reaffirming the primary responsibility of the Transition Government of Mali to protect civilians across its territory in accordance with its international obligations,
Reiterating the centrality of the Agreement on Peace and Reconciliation in Mali emanating from the Algiers process (“the Agreement”) for the achievement of lasting peace in Mali, and welcoming continued efforts of the international mediation under the leadership of Algeria,
Recognizing that the security situation in Mali continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security, and expressing grave concern at continued terrorist attacks and their impact on civilians, including the recent attack in Yarou,
Welcoming the Transition Government of Mali’s commitment to achieve the political transition, organize free and fair presidential elections in February 2024 and ensure the return to constitutional order by March 2024, as endorsed by the ECOWAS Summit of 3 July 2022,
Recalling resolution 2690 (2023) and emphasizing the need for an orderly, safe and well-coordinated withdrawal of the United Nations Multidimensional Integrated Stabilization Mission in Mali (MINUSMA) and the transfer of its tasks, in close consultation with the Transition Government of Mali,
Taking note of the request by the Transition Government of Mali for a review of the sanctions regime, and emphasizing the importance of ensuring that sanctions remain targeted, proportionate and evidence-based and do not impede Mali’s efforts to combat terrorism, protect civilians or deliver humanitarian assistance,
Recognizing the need to support Mali in strengthening its national security and defence forces so that it can assume full responsibility for protecting its territory and civilians,
Stressing the importance of cooperation between the Transition Government of Mali, the Sanctions Committee established pursuant to resolution 2374 (2017) (“the Committee”), the Panel of Experts and all relevant United Nations entities,
Determining that the situation in Mali continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Decides that the measures imposed by paragraphs 1 and 2 of resolution 2374 (2017), as renewed by paragraph 1 of resolution 2649 (2022), shall be extended until 31 August 2024;
2. Reaffirms that the measures referred to in paragraph 1 are intended to target individuals and entities designated by the Committee and are not directed against the people of Mali, and stresses that such measures should not create undue obstacles to the exercise by the Government of Mali of its sovereign responsibility to protect civilians and combat terrorism;
3. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts established by resolution 2374 (2017) until 30 September 2024, and requests the Panel to provide the Council, after discussion with the Committee, with a midterm report no later than 31 March 2024 and a final report no later than 30 September 2024;
4. Requests the Panel of Experts to continue to cooperate closely with the Transition Government of Mali, the Committee and relevant stakeholders, to gather, examine and analyse information on the implementation of the measures, and to ensure that its reporting remains objective, evidence-based and balanced;
5. Calls upon all Member States to cooperate fully with the Committee and the Panel of Experts, including by providing information on the implementation of the measures;
6. Reaffirms its support for the Agreement on Peace and Reconciliation in Mali emanating from the Algiers process, and calls upon all parties to the Agreement to accelerate its implementation and to refrain from any action that could undermine peace and reconciliation;
7. Decides that the measures renewed by this resolution shall be reviewed by 31 March 2024, taking into account progress in the political transition, the implementation of the Agreement and the security situation, with a view to considering the continuation, adjustment or lifting of the measures as appropriate;
8. Calls upon the Transition Government of Mali and all relevant parties to ensure that the withdrawal of MINUSMA and the transfer of its tasks are conducted in an orderly and safe manner and in full respect of the Status of Forces Agreement until the departure of the final element of MINUSMA from Mali;
9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

